;; WHAT EACH ROLE IS, AND WHAT IT MAY TOUCH.
;;
;; Roles used to be one world plus a suffix: every role got the implementer's
;; system prompt — all 31 tools, the REPL-first workflow, how to change code —
;; and then its own prompt argued it back out of the parts that were not its
;; job. The supervisor's role prompt still carries the scar: a whole paragraph
;; explaining that its file tools cannot reach the harness source, which is
;; there because the shared prompt had just told it it has file tools. One
;; supervisor spent 108 of a run's 211 turns hunting a source tree it was
;; never going to be allowed to open (karamazov-i1u).
;;
;; So roles are CONSTRUCTED here rather than subtracted in prose. A role's
;; surface is the tools it may call; the catalogue it is shown is filtered to
;; that surface, and calling outside it is refused rather than discouraged.
;;
;; :tools  the tool surface. :all means every registered tool.
;; :core-tools optional opening presentation only; never changes authorization.
;; :prompt the role prompt appended to the (scoped) system prompt, or nil.
;; :frame  optional prompt that replaces the shared system prompt for this role;
;;         it can place the same sections with {{turn}}, {{tools}}, {{honesty}}.
;; :sees   what its opening context is built from — documentation for the
;;         reader and for the supervisor deciding what to tune; the cells that
;;         assemble each role's context name the same keys.
;;
;; SEPARATE CONTEXTS ARE NOT THE SAME AS SEALED ONES. Every role already runs
;; on its own branch with its own message stream, and that stays true: no role
;; reads another's history directly. The supervisor investigates ACROSS that
;; boundary on purpose — fetch_turn, fetch_artifact, introspect and recall are
;; how it looks at what an implementer did — but what it learns arrives as a
;; tool result in its own stream, which is the difference between reading a
;; record and inheriting a context.
{:implementor
 {:doc "Builds the thing. Guided by its manifests through a task: claim it,
        declare what it will change, explore, land the files, ship. The only
        role that writes to the project."
  ;; EXPLICIT, not `:all`. The catalogue a role is shown is filtered to its
  ;; surface, so `:all` meant the implementor's system prompt carried every
  ;; tool the harness has — measured at 30,625 characters, of which 21,210 was
  ;; the catalogue and 9,000 was the "Changing the harness itself" block
  ;; alone: how to save a cell, version a manifest, edit a policy table,
  ;; rewrite a prompt, reload the loop, introspect its wiring. Nine tools an
  ;; implementor building a game has no use for.
  ;;
  ;; MEASURED BEFORE CUTTING, across runs 498450e1, a3566c73 and f2014821:
  ;; 641 implementor turns, ZERO calls to any of the nine. The block was
  ;; costing every implementor turn and buying nothing. What it displaced is
  ;; visible in the same runs — the TDD instruction sat at character 34,720
  ;; of a 39,595-character system message, behind all of it.
  ;;
  ;; This is the division roles.edn already states in the other direction:
  ;; roles/supervisor.md says the supervisor does not write project code
  ;; because doing so hides the loop problem behind a patch. The converse is
  ;; the same argument — an implementor that retunes the loop mid-task is
  ;; changing the thing it is being measured by, and the supervisor is the
  ;; role with the run-wide view needed to do that responsibly.
  ;;
  ;; `intervene` needs no :denied carve-out any more: an explicit surface
  ;; excludes by absence, which is what made the sentinel need one.
  :tools #{"thesis" "branch_theses" "done" "give_up"
           "plan" "eval" "doc" "complete" "manual" "propose_cell"
           "read_file" "read_digest" "inspect_clojure" "edit_clojure" "apply_clojure_changes" "transform_clojure" "grep" "glob" "lsp" "resource" "inference" "diagnosis" "execution" "structural" "debug" "write_file" "edit_file" "patch"
           "shell" "websearch" "webfetch" "skill"
           "task" "split"
           "remember" "recall" "outcome" "forget" "retire" "message"
           "fetch_artifact" "fetch_turn" "context_edit" "papercut" "finding" "mcp" "wait"
           "tool_search"}
  ;; Evaluation-only dynamic presentation. The complete :tools set above is
  ;; still the authorization boundary; these are the entries documented on the
  ;; opening request. Other roles remain static unless they declare their own
  ;; :core-tools.
  :core-tools #{"thesis" "plan" "done" "give_up" "task" "eval"
                "read_file" "grep" "glob" "write_file" "edit_file" "patch"
                "shell" "tool_search"}
  :prompt "roles/implementor"
  :sees #{:task :problem :shared-tree :skills}}

 :supervisor
 {;; `intervene` is the supervisor's HANDS. Everything else on this surface
  ;; observes the run or tunes the harness for the next one; this is the only
  ;; tool that reaches the run in front of it.
  :doc "The meta process. Does not care how the feature is implemented; cares
        whether the implementer is moving through its steps and what to tune
        when it is not. Reads the run's health, investigates the record, and
        changes the harness — manifests, cells, prompts, policy — so this run
        and the next do better. It does not write project code: that is the
        implementer's job and doing it here hides the loop problem behind a
        patch."
  ;; `websearch` is investigation: a supervisor diagnosing an endpoint or a
  ;; library's behaviour has the same need an implementor does.
  :tools #{"intervene" "read_file" "read_digest" "inspect_clojure" "grep" "lsp" "resource" "inference" "diagnosis" "execution" "skill" "doc" "complete" "manual"
           "introspect" "cells" "cell" "reload_cells" "manifest" "prompt" "policy" "adopt"
           "fetch_turn" "fetch_artifact" "recall" "remember" "forget" "retire" "outcome"
           "experiment" "verdict" "papercut" "battery" "message" "eval" "shell" "websearch" "done" "give_up"}
  :prompt "roles/supervisor"
  ;; Its own frame, so a run's style cannot replace it with the implementer's
  ;; (karamazov-oi16.4): under :lean the supervisor took system-lean, which
  ;; opens "a developer ... make the change the request asks for" and lists the
  ;; implementer's habits, against a 27 KB suffix arguing it back out of the
  ;; work — 32 TB 2.1 passes, 0 workflow changes.
  :frame "supervisor-frame"
  :sees #{:run-health :gate-health :failures}}

 :tester
 {:doc "Writes the tests a hidden grader would write, from the task statement
        alone, and runs them against the deliverable. It never reads the
        implementation, the run's turns or its notes: its value is that it does
        not share the implementer's blind spots (karamazov-galq)."
  ;; No write_file or edit_file: they are confined to the project root, so the
  ;; tester's harnesses landed in the deliverable (ctb-r6 react-lead-form). It
  ;; writes its tests under /tmp/blind-tests with the shell.
  :tools #{"shell" "eval" "read_file" "glob" "done" "give_up"}
  :prompt "roles/tester"
  :output [:map
           [:ran :int]
           [:failing [:vector :string]]]
  :sees #{:requirement}}

 :reviewer
 {:doc "Reads the work against what was asked and returns PASS or REVISE. It
        does not fix what it finds — a reviewer that edits has stopped
        reviewing and the next reviewer inherits its opinion as fact."
  ;; Deliberately NO websearch. A reviewer judges the work against what was
  ;; asked, and going off to read the internet is how a review becomes a
  ;; second opinion about the domain instead of a check on the diff.
  :tools #{"read_file" "read_digest" "inspect_clojure" "grep" "glob" "lsp" "resource" "inference" "doc" "complete" "fetch_artifact"
           "fetch_turn" "recall" "done" "give_up"}
  :prompt "roles/reviewer"
  ;; ITS OUTPUT CONTRACT (karamazov-05z0.14): `done` takes a `result` that
  ;; fits this, and the feature review reads the verdict from it rather than
  ;; from the answer's first line. A malli form; the policy save checks it.
  :output [:map
           [:verdict [:enum "PASS" "REVISE"]]
           [:findings {:optional true} [:vector :string]]]
  :sees #{:requirement :diff :answer}}

 :answerer
 {:doc "Answers a question about the project: reads what it needs and replies.
        The reply is the deliverable — nothing in the project changes, so it
        holds no tool that writes, and no `plan`, whose whole point is naming
        the files a change will touch (karamazov-1wv9)."
  ;; `shell` for the looks a file tool cannot take — `git log`, a directory
  ;; listing, a build file's dependency tree. It is still under the approval
  ;; policy like every other shell call. `eval` is left off: an answer is
  ;; read, not run, and loading a project into the image is its own risk.
  :tools #{"read_file" "read_digest" "inspect_clojure" "grep" "glob" "lsp" "resource" "inference" "diagnosis" "execution" "doc" "shell"
           "websearch" "webfetch" "skill" "manual" "recall" "remember"
           "fetch_turn" "fetch_artifact" "mcp" "done" "give_up"}
  :prompt "roles/answerer"
  :sees #{:problem :skills}}

 :planner
 {:doc "One call: reads the problem, writes the plan and the first task list. Holds
        no tools, and neither do the ideator, manager and summarizer: each is the
        method's fresh context, reading only what the ledger hands it."
  :tools #{}
  :prompt nil
  :sees #{:problem}}

 :ideator
 {:doc "One call: proposes an approach from the technique family its directive
        names, in prose. Never sees the other ideator. Code is stripped from its
        reply, because a later worker implements."
  :tools #{}
  :prompt nil
  :sees #{:problem :plan}}

 :manager
 {:doc "One call per round: reads the ledger and the captured check, curates the
        task list, names the one next task. Mechanical rules bound what it can
        decide: it cannot ship a failing check or send back passing work without
        a counterexample that survives being run."
  :tools #{}
  :prompt nil
  :sees #{:problem :current-work :notes :tasks :latest-result :proposals}}

 :summarizer
 {:doc "One call when a worker is cut off: what it tried and how far it got, for the
        manager to judge."
  :tools #{}
  :prompt nil
  :sees #{:task :attempt}}

 :solver
 {:doc "One worker on one task in a fresh context. Writes the work, runs it, and
        ends with RESULT, NOTES, NEXT and STATUS sections in done's answer. No board,
        plan, memory or harness tools: the manager owns the tasks and the ledger."
  :tools #{"read_file" "read_digest" "grep" "glob" "write_file" "edit_file" "patch"
           "shell" "run_check" "done" "give_up"}
  :prompt "roles/solver"
  :frame "solver-frame"
  :sees #{:problem :plan :notes :current-work :task}}

 :critic
 {:doc "One call, not a loop: the requirement, the diff, and the deterministic
        evidence, in and a verdict out. Holds no tools deliberately — a critic
        that can go and look is a critic that can be talked round by what it
        finds, and the point of it is an opinion formed from the requirement
        and the change alone."
  :tools #{}
  :prompt nil
  :sees #{:requirement :diff :evidence :answer}}}
